From b91bf6f434d5fad38e61a3a2e0f5b9c61450f424 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 22:04:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Keep numeric head/tail heredoc pipelines as
 interpreter data

Why: the heredoc masker rejects every post-opener list operator, including
an ordinary pipe to tail. A nested login shell then scans Python call
arguments with unknown alias names and treats a large SQLite data path as
an executed script. The same program without a pipe was already masked.

Certify only a single heredoc followed by optional 2>&1 and complete
head/tail numeric-filter pipelines, in the executed-path view only.
Unknown tokens, shell sinks, multiple heredocs and explicit consumer/filter
shadowing retain conservative scans. Add head to the shadowable names.
This trusts known command identities under the same policy as existing
interpreter masking; it does not guess arbitrary producer output.

Proof on base 08167653cb:
- Initial regression matrix: 20 RED failures before the fix.
- Final focused suite: 92 passed, including generated data shapes, real
  Bash markers, shell/alias controls and the multi-hop diagnostics shape.
- tests/cron + terminal heredoc guard: 291 files, 12366 passed, 9 skipped.
- Remaining shell_heredoc import consumers: 408 passed.
- scripts/check: 21 checks passed; no health findings.
- Native macOS Bash 3.2 and 5.3: six harmless execution controls each passed.
- Runtime mutants removing the certificate or suffix validation killed.

Separate pre-existing observations are owned by t_ceb128c9b5192804
(function-defined shell sink) and t_3d88573778622ce3 (full original cmp
command includes a later SQLite heredoc; truncated cmp alone is allowed).
